  I've been playing around with the common desktop environment
that Digital supplies as an ADK in their Digital UNIX v3.2C
release.  It's, overall, quite stable and a lot of fun to use.
However, I'm having a problem with a text editor that depends on
ASCII escape sequences that dtterm will not produce.  Basically,
the "Help" and "Do" keys on an LK201 or LK401 keyboard will not
produce an escape sequence.  This seems at variance with the
man page (man 5 dtterm) which claims that F15 and F16 ought
to produce the expected ANSI escape sequences.  These keys do
produce the proper X-window type keystrokes (as expected from
/usr/lib/X11/XKeysymDB).
  The text editor that I use (nu/TPU) expects ANSI escape sequences
for its character cell interface.  The "Do" key (or F16) is fairly
critical to its proper application.  
  Has anyone noticed similar behavior on DEC keyboards with dtterm?
Is there a way to teach these keys their proper escape sequences
(xmodmap seems to apply only to X-window keystrokes)?
  Any help would be appreciated.

