In article <1995Aug29.104915.102326@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu>,
Craig Paul <paul@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu> wrote:
>We'll be moving a heavily accessed, large primary name server to an
>OSF 3.2 box. This summer we AVERAGED 30 I/Os per second during a 10
>day period.
>
>Has anyone stress tested an OSF nameserver to that degree and have
>opinions about it?

We use OSF (currently version 2.0) for our Hesiod servers.  We use either
bind version 4.9.2 or 4.9.3 beta? with about 256,000 resource records.
Memory usage is about 53MB.  Our busiest server averages 32 queries/second.
This is for a network of approximately 1,000 Unix workstations on a campus
with about 10,000 machines total.

This is on three model 3000/300's that also handle some other services.
Our only real problems have been due to the sheer number of resource
records and related memory usage.  You have to make sure you have enough
physical memory or things get real slow.

For comparison, our campus-wide name servers run on Decstation 2100's
with 16MB of RAM.  They have about 70,000 resource records and use
about 12MB.  The busiest server averages 13 queries/second.  These machines
only do name service and are not anywhere near busy.

Name service just isn't very resource intensive.
