X-Deja-AN: 109083562
references: <41bm25$ade@nile.intac.com> <41gutk$1nrq@thebes.waikato.ac.nz> <41jcma$79o@fang.dsto.defence.gov.au>
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

I do have experience with Digital Unix (OSF/1 3.2), HP-UX 9.05 and
Solaris 2.3. IMHO, Digital Unix is the best of all.

1) administration is BSD'ish
2) it is *very* stable
3) contrary to what has been said, all my file systems are ADVFS's, and
   we didn't encounter any problems yet.
4) for software development (and using/compiling PD software), it's very
   convenient. All the libraries are where You expect them to be, X11 is
   very complete (including Imake etc - versus e.g. the HP's)

One negative point is, that You need at least 48 or 64 MB of memory to
run it at acceptable speed. 32 MB just swaps and swaps and swaps and ...
But as I understand, V3.2C should run without swaping and swaping and
(see above) also on 32MB systems - I hadn't the chance to get my hands
on it yet...

By the way, I manage 5 OSF systems, one a dual-processor server with
128MB memory and about 50 GB of disks, all ADVFS (and RAID)...

~a





