In article <421tbj$4sd@usenet.ucs.indiana.edu>,
Alec Habig <ahabig@bigbang.astro.indiana.edu> wrote:
>Lukas Rosenthaler <rosenth@foto.chemie.unibas.ch> wrote:
>>I do have experience with Digital Unix (OSF/1 3.2), HP-UX 9.05 and
>>Solaris 2.3. IMHO, Digital Unix is the best of all.
>
>HPUX has two strong points that OSF lacks, however :
>
>1) SAM.  Some things are simply easier to do from SAM, and for tasks that
>   you've never done before, you can often get away with rooting through menu's
>   in SAM instead of trying to get all the parameters set and configs edited
>   from the command line.  AIX's SMIT is even nicer - it leaves you a log of
>   what happened in a script file format, so that once you've done something
>   one time from the menus, you can do it right from the command line in the
>   future (that being what god intended when he invented unix, of course :)

Believe it or not, I'd actually consider this a weak point.. Because
HP-UX is managed by a GUI tool, the config files and startup scripts
(at least in HP-UX 9.x, I've not looked at 10 yet) are an incredibly
ugly mess, and nearly impossible for a human to read and alter if need
be.  Digital Unix, by contrast, has the cleanest config files and
startup scripts I've ever seen.  Most system changes can be made by
editing one clean, well documented file - /etc/rc.config.


>2) Software support.  When HP comes out with a new version of HPUX, you can
>   install the upgrade across the net if you like.  

I'm not sure I understand.  If you mean that you mount the CD on your
server, and install clients over a local net, then Digital Unix can do
this too via RIS.  In fact, when I install a new machine, or upgrade
an existing one, I just boot it diskless over the net via the RIS
kernel, and mirror an existing machine with dump/restore.  This way, I
need to edit 3 files, and I have a working machine configured
properly.. No wading through a vendor's install script ;-)


>						  Also, as bugs are fixed,
>   you can grab the patches right from HP, in a very convienient fashion.

You're right there.  For all the hype about having patches available
over the net, they're are only about 4 or 5 unique patches available
for Digital Unix over the net. *sigh*

>HPUX's weak points when compared to OSF - 
>
>1) SYSV
>
>2) the VUE GUI interface is too smart for its own good - it's always trying to
>   do things for you, whether you want them done or not.  And heaven help you
>   if you try to figure out where it's stashing all that info.

Just go build the MIT X11 clients, and add/uncomment the following
line in /usr/vue/config/Xconfig :

Vuelogin*xdmMode:           True

Now your HP is running xdm with MIT X clients, and you've gotten rid
of the darned VUE junk.

One more thing to add -- I think Digital Unix has a *huge* advantage
over HP-UX because of NFS V3.  When moving from V2 to V3 of NFS (OSF/1
2.x -> OSF/1 3.0), I saw a 300%-600% performance increase for
NFS-write operations, and about 150% increase in performance for
NFS-read operations.  If course, if you've got a standalone machine,
or use AFS, then you could care less about this..

Drew

