> Greg Robinson <robinson@dsto.defence.gov.au> wrote:
> >*My* next choice is DU 3.2.  As I'm a BSD man from way back, anything
> >SysV'ish makes me shiver.  I would place Solaris next, AIX last.

> Huh?!

> I treat Digital Unix (all versions that I've dealt with) just like System V.

Heh. Did you notice that "reboot" or "halt" don't run "/sbin/rc0.d/K*"? It's
still running the BSD init. Did you notice /sys/conf/KERNEL instead of
/etc/conf/cf.d/mdevice and /etc/conf/sdevice.d?

> Everything works as expected. I can't stand BSD. We evaluated Solaris 2.3,
> and it was so BSDish - and we couldn't turn off the BSDisms enough to get
> things to work - that we finally decided on OSF/1 1.3 instead.

The problem with Solaris is that if you want to do things the BSD way, you
can't turn off the System V isms, and if you want to do things the System
V way, you can't turn off the BSDisms. Digital UNIX does things the BSD
way internally, where it counts, and presents a clean System V administration
interface. An ideal compromise.
