Online Documentation, Tutorials, Context Sensitive Reference and
Hardcopy for us old folk ;^)

>Here is a specific question for starters:

>One manual is "Client Installation and Configuration Guide" and
>another is "Microsoft LAN Manager Installation and Configuration
>Guide". I know what Pathworks is and I know it is based on LAN
>Manager, 

PATHWORKS for DOS and Windows is based on MS-NET, LAN Manager &
Novell's NetWare with a smattering of Infoserver/LAD access, Terminal
Emulation for DOS & Windows, LAT, TCP/IP connectivity, PATHWORKS
Mosaic, PC DECwindows/Motif for DOS, ManageWORKS, DCE middleware
(RPC, InfoBroker, ObjectBroker, DECmessageQ ...) and more.

>now what is the LAN Manger Installation guide for? Is
>it just for extra reading material or is it a separate product.

It is for reference - since you can set up a PATHWORKS client with
an Enhanced Redirector we're talking LAN Manager - it would be nice
to have some [horses mouth] LAN Manager reference without having
to buy THAT documentation from another vendor (Microsoft) or have
to take a Microsoft LAN Manager type course (hey, I just want to use
PATHWORKS :^) - BEWARE - chunks of the EXCELLENT LAN Manager
Documentation included refer to the OS/2-based server - so don't pay
too much close attention to that if you're using an OpenVMS or Digital
Unix (as in your case) server.  Great LANMAN.INI reference !!!!
Watch out - LAN Manager and DOS-based application information
will not be found in the Information Shelf.  Makes sense - those are
Microsoft's books and having Windows-based Help to support DOS
apps might be a bit presumptuous :^)

Okay, I know it's coming - so where's the NOVELL client/server
documentation?  I guess they felt that if you want a PATHWORKS client
to buy you connectivity to a Novell world, then you already have the
documentation for your [existing] site. Besides, what's to a NetWare
client anyway? (about 5 files - badabing!). Whereas EXISTING PATHWORKS
V4 sites moving from MS-NET to LAN Manager need all the reference
they can get, because of the RADICALLY difference extended features
available to the Enhanced Redirector client (browsing for shares, DOMAIN
logon, remote server admin, support for multiple transports ...)

There are a LOT of Microsoft LAN Manager apps that come with a PATHWORKS
client - check out NETADMIN for instance - have fun.

Paul Johnston
Digital Equipment of Canada - Learning Services, Toronto
Opinions expressed are my own, but I'm willing to share.



