stevew@dougal.logica.co.uk (Steve Wyeth) wrote:
> engj@u.washington.edu (Jimmy Eng) writes:
>>
>> The problem is that one of the three workstations won't allow
>> rsh/rlogin to work (i.e. I get "permission denied" or a password
>> is required).  So, any idea what my problem is?  Are there
>> other files that control remote access besides the $USER/.rhosts
>> or /etc/hosts.equiv files? 
> 
> I've just seen that for myself... We had 5 machines, all with pretty
> similar configurations but one machine couldn't rsh to one of the
> others. All other combinations (including the reverse) was fine.
> In the end, the problem disappeared by removing the entry for
> the client in the server's /etc/hosts file (we're using DNS as well,
> so it made no difference - the IP address in the hosts file was
> correct, by the way).

Are the hostnames listed in their short form or their fully qualified
form?  That seems to make a difference in some implementations of UNIX
(IBM's AIX comes to mind).  And Digital UNIX is sensitive about the
order in which these are listed in the /etc/hosts file (list the full
name first, then the short name and any nicknames the host has).  This
would help explain why removing a entry from /etc/hosts might fix 
things -- if the entry was in the wrong order and rsh consulted it, it
might not work, whereas a DNS entry would return a fully qualified
hostname that works OK.

I tell people here to have two .rhosts entries for each machine, one
in the short form, one in the fully qualified form, and that seems
to satisfy our heterogeneous network's rsh needs.

(Definitions, in case you need them:  the "short form" is just the
single hostname (in our case, "odin"); a "fully qualified" name is
with the network part attached, like "odin.mda.uth.tmc.edu".)

