keywords: logical filesystems, disk striping
nntp-posting-user: bicknell
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <41bo71$b5q@amy26.Stanford.EDU>,
Bilal Ahmad <ahmadpk@leland.stanford.edu> wrote:
>	I would like to know if anyone has evaluated, or knows of some
>numbers otherwise, the performance gain that one gets by using the logical
>filesystems and spreading them across several disks to enhance I/O speed.

	We had 3 rz57's (old DEC, full height 5.25" 1 gig drives)
here, each with a 512M /u{1,2,3} parition on it.  We took those
partitions and combined them into one striped /home 1.5 gig partition.
We have been very happy with it.

	In testing, we got 2.5M/second write performace to the
/home system, rather remarkable considering the slow old drives.
Also, it's nice that user load is always evenly distributed over
all the drives.  Before it was not uncommon for 60% of the users
online at any given time to be on one home parition.

	The only drawback is that if one of those drives fail,
you lose the whole partition.  Since we do regular backups and 
have had good luck with the drives, and since it's not a 
mission critical computer that's not a big deal to us.  It might
be to you.

-- 
