>
>Since there does not seem to be an appropriate entry in our /etc/disktab 
>file, I thought that I'd consult the experts on this newsgroup :-)

The disklabel command can get the geometry from the drive and a
default partition table from the drive.  It won't get the correct
rotation speed unless it happens to be 3600 rpm, but this is
probably only interesting if you want use a non-zero rotdelay
on UFS.

Use the disklabel command first:

# disklabel -wr /dev/rrz#c st32550n

Then if you want to change anything use disklabel with the -e
option.  With a label on the disk, newfs can get of the information
it needs from there.  No disktab entry needed.

