In article <40nmpd$t2l@infoserv.rug.ac.be>, Christophe Colle <colle> writes:
|>Hi,
|>
|>I'm new to c++. I just installed cxx and g++. When I try to compile following
|>code i get some linking errors.. When I compile I compile the C version 
|>with gcc and cc, there is no error. What is wrong?
|>
|>code :
|>#include <unistd.h>
|>#include <sys/types.h>
|>#include <netinet/in.h>
|>#include <sys/time.h>
|>#include <sys/socket.h>
|>#include <arpa/inet.h>
|>#include <netdb.h>
|>
|>
|>main()
|>{
|>   struct hostent *ptr=gethostbyname((char *)0l);
|>
|>}
|>
|>colle@krtkg1> g++ t.cc
|>/usr/bin/ld:
|>Unresolved:
|>gethostbyname__FPCc
|>collect2: ld returned 1 exit status
|>colle@krtkg1> cxx t.cc
|>t.cc:18: warning: The procedure "main" returns a non-void value but does not
|>contain a return statement.
|>ld:
|>Error: Undefined:
|>gethostbyname__XPCc
|>

This is due to the fact that the compiler mangled the name of 'gethostbyname'
for type-safe linkage, as described in your basic C++ text.  The problem
is with netdb.h not being C++-safe on your version of the o/s.  You don't 
say what version of Digital UNIX you are running, by my 3.2 system has
C++-safe header files.  If you only want to declare gethostbyname, then
you should say:

extern "C" {
extern struct hostent *gethostbyname __((const char *));
}

Regards,
Tim

