organization: Conservatoire National des Arts et Metiers
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



On Tuesday 8 August 95, at 8 h 58, the keyboard of bortzmeyer@cnam.fr (Stephane Bortzmeyer) wrote:

> I'm experimenting with ping on an Alpha (DEC 3000/600S) with 
> OSF/1 2.0. Apparently, ping round-trip times are always 
> multiples of 0.977 ms (1 second / 1024...). I don't see the 
> same behaviour on Linux, for instance.
> 
> Replacing gettimeofday by getclock, as suggested by the man 
> page apparently changes nothing.
> 
> Does anyone know how to obtain a better time resolution (one 
> milli-second is clearly too long for an Alpha on Ethernet)?

Well, as explained by Jeffrey Mogul <mogul@pa.dec.com> and a few others, 
IP delivers packets when they arrive, not at discrete intervals. getclock 
changes nothing because it's just another library routine on the top of 
gettimeofday. According to Jeffrey Mogul, "Sooner or later, perhaps in 
the next major release, Digital UNIX [the new name of OSF/1] will sup-
port microsecond resolution in gettimeofday()." (Linux already has
it.)

In the mean time, there exist an (unsupported) way to reach that pre-
cision: directly read the program counter of the Alpha. Here is Jef-
frey Mogul's code to do that:

--------- readpcc.s -----------------
#define v0	$0
#define ra	$26
#define	sp	$30
#define zero	$31

	.align 4
        .globl  ReadPCC
        .ent    ReadPCC,0
ReadPCC:
        .frame  sp,0,ra
	rpcc	v0			# read the process cycle counter
	ret	zero,(ra)
	.end	ReadPCC
--------- end of readpcc.s -----------------

To use it, you first calibrate the processor and then you use that 
routine to improve gettimeofday results. As far as I know, the full 
code is not redistributable but it's quite simple.

I've used this improved gettimeofday in my traceroute Web page:

http://web.cnam.fr/Network/traceroute.html

which indicates the speeds of the various links encountered.

Thanks to everybody and specially to Jeffrey Mogul <mogul@pa.dec.com>,
Eric Werme USG <werme@zk3.dec.com> and Dave Cherkus <cherkus@UniMaster.COM>.

