sender: ahmadpk@leland.Stanford.EDU
organization: Stanford University
keywords: logical filesystems, disk striping
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Hi!

	I would like to know if anyone has evaluated, or knows of some
numbers otherwise, the performance gain that one gets by using the logical
filesystems and spreading them across several disks to enhance I/O speed.

	We have an AlphaServer 2000 4/200 running OSF/1 V3.2. There are
three user filesystems and three physical disks. The physical disks are
striped to form three logical disks. The user filesystems are on these
logical disks. Due to the potential difficulties in case of a disk failure,
we are considering not to use disk striping and limit each filesystem to
a physical disk. But we have no idea of how much performance we'll be
giving up.

	My intuition suggests that if the swap space is spread across
disks, equally. Then if we are not reading/writing huge amounts of
data files very often, the performance benefit of spreading filesystems
across physical disks might not be too great. Please comment!

Bilal




