> We have a DECstation 5000 model 200, Ultrix V4.4 system.  To back up to our 
> HP35480 4 Gb DAT drive, we do an auto shutdown late at night, then use the 
> dump command in single user mode to get our backups.  We will need to move to> a backup strategy which doesn't require shutdown to single user mode soon, 
> preferably one which doesn't involve backing up across the network, for speed> reasons.  I have two questions :
>
> 1)  Does OSF/1 have the same restriction of using dump only in single user 
> mode ?

Neither Ultrix or OSF/1 have the restriction of using dump in single
user mode.  DEC has a lousy belief that only root can run dump.  Many
sites (including mine) like to use a designated user for backups.  The
only reason to bring the machine to single user mode is if you're
worried about lots of inodes changing while you are doing the backups.
I haven't had any trouble and have decided that it's an acceptable
risk.

> 2) What do other Unix sites use (especially Ultrix or OSF/1 sites) to get 
> reliable backups while in multi-user mode ?
>

For Ultrix and OSF/1, dump and rdump are setuid root and restricted to
one group that includes the dump user.  SunOS doesn't have this
limitation.

