One of the claimed advantages of 3.2C is that it works better in
limited memory.  There was a question as to whether that represented a
real improvement, or whether there was some special stripped-down 24
MB configuration.  I just got a copy of 3.2C.  Since it's the released
version, I don't think I'm under any secrecy agreements...  There
seems to be a combination of things going on.  There is a special
configuration for systems under 32 MB.  It removes NFS server and some
other things.  My system is a 32 MB 3000/300LX, so it doesn't get the
special configuration.  It still behaves a lot better than under 3.2.
I don't claim to know what has been done, but I don't think any
functionality has been omitted.  It feels like the change from Solaris
2.3 to 2.4, where my 24 MB SparcClassic suddenly became usable.

I've been playing with CDE.  I found that in order to get liveable
performance under 3.2 I had to use xterm (rather than CDE's dtterm)
and emacs 18.  With 3.2C, I can use dtterm and emacs19, and things
start up reasonably.  That's not to say that CDE has suddenly stopped
being a pig: it hasn't.  But it's now no more of a pig than I'd expect
based on experiences on Suns and larger Digital Unix machines.



