Chua Koon Teck (koonteck@merlion.singnet.com.sg) wrote:
: I understand that there can be different level to a vdump backup.  If I 
: will to use level 0 to vdump a fileset to a tape, such as
: 
: vdump -0u -f /dev/nrmt0h /var
: 
: if I will to do a level 5 to vdump the same fileset to the same tape, 
: such as
: 
: vdump -5u -f /dev/nrmt0h /var
: 
: Will the second vdump be appended to the level 0 data which is already in 
: the tape ?  How can I use the vrestore command to see the level 0 and 
: level 5 backup in the tape ?

If you get a tape with level 0 dumped, put it to streamer and try to
write another dump (e.g. level 5), the dump will begin from the beginning
of the tape, erasing the previously written contents.
The way to omit this is the 'mt' command.

E.g.

mt rewind - rewinds the tape to the begining
then you can do
mt fsf 1 - and you move 1 file forward, so your tape is set at the position
           just after the end-of-file (here end of 0 level dump)
now you can make a level 5 dump.

The same way you can restore the particular dump.

