X-Deja-AN: 108079902
sender: usenet@uns.bris.ac.uk (Usenet news owner)
references: <bhunt.807871900@brians.umd.edu> <bhunt.808006343@brians.umd.edu> <40fsbp$lra@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>
organization: Bristol High Energy Physics
reply-to: dave.newbold@bristol.ac.uk
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Hi,

According to the manpage, sincos has a type of 'double complex', and it mentions
that the routine can only be used by languages that support that type.

At a guess, this means that you can:

 - Call it from fortran, which has a 'complex*16' type. You'll need to write
   a C 'buffer' routine to pass the argument, as sincos wants its argument
   passed by value, not by reference. Be careful of the function prototypes
   in <math.h>, or you may get unwanted type conversion.

 - The fortran docs state that the complex*16 type is stored as two
   consecutive real*8. So define a simple structure to mimic the double_complex
   type. Again, could be problems with prototypes in the header file.

As somebody mentioned, I think the compiler recognizes apt situations to use
sincos - make sure you make it 'obvious' that you are taking the cos, sin of
the same thing (use the same variable as an argument?).

