Well, that's probably a function of the TCP/IP stack that is on each
OS.  I do know that there is a physical limitation relating to time
and transmit/return speeds.  For instance, there is a standard 1ms
latency associated with transmittal of an IP packet across long
distance networks, typically 1ms=100km.  So, for every 100km of
distance a packet must travel, there is a 1ms latency observed, this
goes for both a transmission as well as the acknowledgment (ACK).
Thats why many of the IP over ATM tests that have transpired using,
for instance, T3 circuits (44.736 Mbps) usually post 25 Mbps
throughput, maximum.  This relates to the "windowing" issue, or the
MTU (Max. Transmit. Unit) controversy about which is the best size for
long dist. transmission.






