I would have said more if I was more certain about what I had to say.
So, with lotsa caveats:

The Boot PROMs are a little code and a lot of compressed data that is
expanded into RAM.  A lot of the console code (including the callbacks
used for crash dumps and (possibly?) console I/O) lives in that RAM.
Also, all the PALcode, instructions that trap to code that has special
system-specific hardware access, live in that RAM too.

PALcode is used for breakpoints, system calls, interrupt management, and
access to hardware counters.

I have no idea how much of the 2 MB actually needs to be around while
the OS is running.
