When you have mission critical applications that can cost you thousands of
dollars a second when they are down, $8000 for a compiler that is SUPPORTED is
not that much to pay.  Who do you call at GNU when you have a problem at 3 in
the morning that has to be fixed, NOW?

If I was doing research at a university or teaching, you bet I would be using
the freeware.  But, not everyone is in that situation.  Economic prinicples
have to be applied to each situation.  And if you are down for a minute or two
at the stock exchange, for example, that 8G's you saved gets burned up in a
hurry and your boss is asking you hard questions about your application of
'basic economic principles' and your total obliviousness to reality.

BK

