I'm getting some weird behavior from our new mail server's sendmail.
(It's an DEC AlphaServer 1200 Model 5/250 running OSF/1 v3.2B)
Here's how the system's sendmail.cf defines some things:

   ##########################
   ###   Special macros   ###
   ##########################

   # UNIX header format
   DlFrom $g  $d
   # format of a total name
   Dq$?x$x $.<$g>

   #############################
   ###   Format of headers   ###
   #############################

   H?F?From: $q

But a typical header on our outgoing mail sometimes looks like this:

   From dct@odin.mda.uth.tmc.edu  Tue Aug 15 14:03:44 1995
   From: "David C. Tuttle" <dct@odin>

When I edit the Dq line to say "Dq$?x$x $.<$g.$N>" to force putting a
fully qualified hostname, it comes out:

   From: "David C. Tuttle" <dct@odin.mda.uth.tmc.edu.mda.uth.tmc.edu>

I remove the $N, and it goes back to "<dct@odin>".  All the other
headers look fine, including the "From " line which also uses $g.

I'm using DEC's mailsetup utility, then banging on sendmail.cf until
the headers are just right.  Unfortunately, it seems like I've got the
problem licked, only to have it recur a few days later (no reboot, no
restart of sendmail, it just happens).  Am I missing something here?
Is there any way to modify (or even just test) the $g macro?  Anyone
who has any information on this is welcome to reply to me.  Thanks.

