Yoonsuck Choe <yschoe@bullwinkle.che.utexas.edu> wrote:
>
>I have a DEC alpha 3000/400 with DEC OSF/1 V3.2 (32M physucal mem), 
>which is extremely slow these days.
>
>It seems that it continuously swaps in and swaps out pages
>when I run a large (about 30 to 40M) program.

Well, yes - (32M real memory - 40 MB application) = at least 8MB of swapping,
even with nothing else (including the system) going on.

>The swapping starts when RES goes over 15M.

Ok.  So your system stuff is taking up about 17MB, which seems fair.

>I found that the process 0 was taking up 185M of VSZ (ps augx):
>
>USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TTY      S  STARTED  TIME     COMMAND
>root         0  0.0 51.8  185M  325 ??       R < Jul 13  01:23:32 [kernel
>								   idle]
>It is not taking up a large real memory (325) though.

The kernel claims to be taking up all your swap space+, all the time.  It's not
really doing that of course, that's just the way it talks to ps.  For example,
on my machine w/128MB RAM, here's what it says :

  PID USER       VSZ  RSS %MEM        TIME COMMAND
    0 root      289M  119  8.6     0:33.08 [kernel idle]

>Can this be related to the slow-down?
>If not, what should I do to diagmose this problem?

Don't try to run executables bigger than your physical RAM and not expect
swapping!

Something that would help - don't run anything else (especially X) while
running your big program.  It'll still swap, of course, but hopefully not so
much. 

	Alec

