In <40ar4c$o2i@geraldo.cc.utexas.edu> yschoe@bullwinkle.che.utexas.edu (Yoonsuck Choe) writes:

>I have a DEC alpha 3000/400 with DEC OSF/1 V3.2 (32M physucal mem), 
>which is extremely slow these days.
>
>It seems that it continuously swaps in and swaps out pages
>when I run a large (about 30 to 40M) program.
>The swapping starts when RES goes over 15M.
>
>I found that the process 0 was taking up 185M of VSZ (ps augx):
>
>USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TTY      S  STARTED  TIME     COMMAND
>root         0  0.0 51.8  185M  325 ??       R < Jul 13  01:23:32 [kernel
>								   idle]
>It is not taking up a large real memory (325) though.

I disagree.  Look at the %MEM field.  You can ignore the VSZ and RSS
fields, process 0 is not a real process.  But the kernel does use (a lot
of) physical memory.

>Can this be related to the slow-down?

No.

>If not, what should I do to diagmose this problem?

There is very little you can do if you want to run a 40M program on an
OSF/1 system with 32M of RAM.  Either reduce the size of your program
or increase the size of your RAM.  The only magic solution is to keep
all your memory references local, if possible.

Dan
