In article <40br6e$knf@merlin.resmel.bhp.com.au>, Joe Spanicek,BHP-Research PKL,, <joe@holly.resptk.bhp.com.au> writes:
|>Hi,
|>
|>I have OSF/1 V3.0 using "DEC C" & "DEC C++ V1.3B". The problem I am having is
|>calling a archive compiled with 'C++' from with C code witch is compiled with
|>'C'.
|>
|>The problem lies with linking the archive library to the generated C code. The
|>C++ compiler produces object code with a 'name' reference to functions
|>differently than does the C compiler. So in the end the linker can not make
|>the connection between the functions.
|>
|>Could anyone give pointers on how to get around this, I am fluent in C but just
|>started in C++. I am trying to use a library written in C++ within software
|>written only in C.
|>

Declare swapme in function.cc as `extern "C"':

#include <stdio.h>

extern "C" void	// <<----------------------------------------------
swapme(int *p, int *q)
{
printf("in swapme: %d %d\n", *p, *q);
	int t = *p;
	*p = *q;
	*q = t;
}

Michael Spratte
