In article <danpop.807801735@rscernix>, Dan.Pop@mail.cern.ch (Dan Pop) says:
>
>It depends on your definition for "official name".  For me, "official" is
>whatever string is reported by uname, and this hasn't changed.
>
>The "marketing" name has changed, indeed, to "Digital UNIX" and the reasons
>are purely political: UNIX sounds better than OSF/1 and DEC prefers to be
>called Digital these days.  The logical result is "DEC OSF/1" being
>renamed "Digital UNIX".

The name change involves a little bit more than just the wish of a few
marketing people to now call this thing 'Digital UNIX'. 
X/Open Co. Ltd. in spring 95 announced ist licensing program for the 
UNIX trademark. Operating system products branded as 
conformant to the Single UNIX Specification (formerly Spec 1170) will 
be able to carry the X/Open UNIX brand.
We submitted DEC OSF/1 to the branding program and got the brand around
march/april. This brand for us carries the right to use the word UNIX 
in our o/s name. The result we all know. 

