I'm assuming it's differnt as well on the AS 8x00 machines as well
since they are newer than the 4000 series machines.  As I said above
though, take what I say with a grain of salt since I've never used the
AS 8x000 systems, though I'd love to.  :]

As Joe and I say, use doconfig and follow the instructions in the man
page to configure the system properly.  Though unless you have all the
possible SCSI devices installed, it doesn't include them by default.
I like to edit the new config file to have all possible RZ and TZ
devices defined to the controllers I have in the system.  This makes
it much simpler to add devices to a system without having to rebuild
the kernel each time.

Thinking about it, why should we have to bother adding device entries
for each controller?  The kernel should know that each SCSI bus can
have 8 devices of various types and just allocate the needed
structures dynamically.  Then all we'd have to worry about it adding
the correct controller entry(s) when adding new hardware.

