In article <JOHN.95Aug8223538@avante.wpi.edu>,
John Stoffel <john@avante.WPI.EDU> wrote:
>Thinking about it, why should we have to bother adding device entries
>for each controller?  The kernel should know that each SCSI bus can
>have 8 devices of various types and just allocate the needed
>structures dynamically.  Then all we'd have to worry about it adding
>the correct controller entry(s) when adding new hardware.

Careful--the host-side view of a SCSI bus can have more than 8 devices.
For example, our fancy new HSZ40 array can present up to 56 separate
disks that appear at the host side on one SCSI bus. It does this by
mapping (SCSI bus, SCSI ID) pairs on the HSZ40 side to (SCSI ID, LUN)
pairs on the host side. I know all about the joys of calculating lots
of fun device numbers/minor numbers/device names because 3.2c can still
only auto-detect the disks with LUN 0. On the other hand, you're
right that there ought to more dynamic auto-detecting of things, but
it's probably being worked on.

