Date: 1995/08/06
Message-ID: <4023ls$j7r@news.kth.se>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 107556460
references: <3v8n7o$avs@news.kth.se> <3vs40u$9dn@metro.ucc.su.OZ.AU>
content-type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
organization: Dept. of Atomic Physics, University of Stockholm, Sweden
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

A while a go I asked:

> Is it in any way possible to use an ordinary terminal (like a vt100)
> as console of this machine?
[this machine = 250 4/266]

I got several replies. Thank you all! The following I got from Kenneth A.
House (house@eng.pko.dec.com) at DEC. (It's slightly edited by me.)

    The information below will work for any of the AlphaStation 200, 250,
    400 platforms.

    Plug the VT100 into the COM1 port, using 9600 BAUD, 8-bit characters,
    one stop bit, no parity.  You'll need an adapter to go from the VT100's
    25-pin connector to the AlphaStation's 9-pin connector.

    Power on, and you should see the Console messages on your serial
    terminal.  Press the Return key a time or two to get the Console's
    attention, then type

    	set console serial
    	init

    at the Console prompt ( three angle brackets, like this >>> ).

    To get back to using the graphics controller as the Console device, the
    magic incantation is

    	set console graphics
    	init

[End of quote]

We tried this and it worked very well. The 9-to-25 pin DSUB adapter can be
of the simple three wire type. The terminal should already be switched on
when the Alpha boots. Thanks again everybody!


Regards,
		Jonas

-- 
