organization: Rutgers University LCSR
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

s11976@net2.hkbu.edu.hk (Wong Pui Ming) writes:

>One of the DEC staff told me that the 'official' name of DEC OSF/1
>has been renamed as "DIGITAL UNIX". Is it true ? If so, any political
>reason for this change ?

I'll give you my impression from reading between the lines of the
announcements: I don't know whether it's still true, but at one time
there was a perception in the market that OSF1 somehow wasn't "real
Unix".  As one of the first vendors to qualify under the new standards
for Unixness (Unixicity?), Digital thought that this was a good time
to emphasize that its Unix product is just as real as anyone else's.
I suspect they are also concerned about OSF stopping future
development of OSF1.  Thus they want to emphasize that while they
started with OSF1, Digital Unix is now being developed by Digital, and
it is not be limited by what OSF did or didn't do.

In fact I'd say that I find porting software to Digital Unix about as
easy as to other versions I know of.  The main problem these days is
the 64-bit architecture.  But everybody's going to have to go through
that, and frankly it hasn't been as much of a problem as I expected it
to be.  There are a few implementations of Unix that I consider
problematical (no, I'm not going to tell you which ones they are -- we
try to maintain the good will of lots of vendors), but Digital Unix
isn't one of them.  My main reservation at the moment is that 32 MB
isn't quite enough for a workstation.  That's supposed to be fixed in
3.2C, which is due out any moment.




