>for the string *string*.  then, if it finds it,
>it would display the line containing the string AND
>three lines directly above it, and three lines 
>directly below it.  any help?

Use GNU grep instead: the option -3 displays 3 lines either side of
each match. If you want an asymmetric window then you can use, e.g.,
-A 4 -B 5 to display 4 lines after and 5 lines before each match.

You'll also find that in its "fixed string" incarnation, it doesn't
have the same bugs as the fgrep supplied in OSF/1 3.0. It might be
fixed in 3.2c but I doubt it.
    % echo abacababacababc | fgrep abacababc
    % echo abaabaac | fgrep abaac
    % 
(actually these and similar bugs are present in most BSD and SysV
fgreps but, interestingly, they have different bugs. OSF/1 shows
the same bug as the SysV fgrep whereas Ultrix shows the same bug
as the BSD one.)

--Malcolm

