From: mogul@pa.dec.com (Jeffrey Mogul)
Subject: Performance effect of -xtaso
Date: 1995/08/01
Message-ID: <3vjtg5$a55@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 107284847
references: <1995Jul27.222628.16709@catfish.az05.bull.com>
organization: DEC Western Research
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <1995Jul27.222628.16709@catfish.az05.bull.com> andyw@sickbay.az05.bull.com (Andy Wild ) writes:
>Secondly what is the performance effect of running with -taso rather than
>true 64-bit. 

I do not believe that using -taso will significantly affect performance,
since it simply changes the base address of the program.  However,
I've never measured this.

The -xtaso switch, on the other hand, when used in conjunction with
the appropriate #pragma constructs, causes pointers to be kept in
32 bit fields instead of 64 bit fields.  (For this reason, -xtaso
programs MUST be linked with -taso.)

This can affect performance, but it's impossible to give you a
single number.  We wrote a paper on this, which appeared in
Proc. 1995 USENIX Conference (January, 1995), titled "Performance
Implications of Multiple Pointer Sizes."  You can get a copy
via the Web at

	http://www.research.digital.com/wrl/techreports/abstracts/94.7.html

Please read this before asking more questions!

-Jeff



