  I'm working with OSF/1 for i386. My computer is a 486 and the OSF/1 version
is: OSF1 1.2 osc1.2-MK5.0.2 i386. I'm working with Mach 3.0, writing clients
and servers which run in differents computers (linked by a network). I'm
trying to install  "netmsgserver", a name server which it comes with the
standard installation. This server can send messages by the network.

   To install properly "netmsgserver" there are to take into account how
Mach 3.0 works. The "Mach 3.0 Server Writer's Interfaces", appendix C
Service Server, explains very well this point. This handook says:

   Each task inherits a set of registered ports upon creation
(mach_port_registers). These are (send rights) for the name server, an
environment server and the service server. All tasks in the system are
intented to share the same name server, whereas different groups of tasks may
have private environmet servers.

   All tasks need access to the same name server. Given a task whose
registered name server port names the name server, all tasks derived from
that task will inherit the port and share access to the name server.
Unfortunately, being a task itself, the name server intializes itself after
certain other tasks (such as the init process, /etc/init) run. Thus, most
tasks, which derive indirectly from the init task will be missing the name
server (and environmment server) port.

  Because of this, the port for the name server and environment server
must be created (and registered with the init process) before the servers
the ports name exist. This mechanism is provided by the "service" server.

  The privileged server is "mach_init". I installed "mach_init" in
"/mach_servers", and therefore, when the operating system starts, runs
"/mach_init". I did some "ls -l"'s to some important programs:

